2022 was a year of weather Extremes in  the UK likely according to the Met  Office to have been the warmest on  record  it meant heat waves and destructive  wildfires  and a punishing drought that sucked the  life out of rivers and lakes  there were powerful storms too and  widespread flooding that followed  at the end of the year bought a cold  snap and a deep freeze not all of this  was driven by climate change but much of  it was we can't tackle and adapt to  climate change without restoring nature  now the National Trust is warning that  what could be the new normal presents a  real challenge for our natural world  they've had a really tough year and  we've seen whole grassland Landscapes  like places like it around Stonehenge  where the grass and the flowers have  just died back very prematurely and that  of course has knock-on impacts on things  like the pollinators like butterflies  and bumblebees which are reliant on  those plants you're losing habitat and  food sources for the Wildlife and so it  Cascades down these ecosystems among the  species that suffered were bats who  couldn't cope with the extra heat  some butterflies and other flying  insects struggled too as habitats were  impacted by the drought  on top of this a virulent strain of bird  flu devastated seabird colonies despite  the mainly bad news for nature a few  success stories have also emerged with  some wildlife and habitat showing that  with a little assistance they can adapt  well to the new conditions  that includes these beavers benefiting  from a project to protect their wet  woodland habitat in Somerset  and these chuffs are slowly starting to  thrive in Cornwall after successful  efforts to protect them  the natural world demonstrating both its  fragility and resilience as our weather  becomes more unpredictable and extreme  Hannah Thomas Peter Sky News
 

